1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,200
Four cars arrive at a crossroads at
exactly the same moment. No lights.

2
00:00:05,320 --> 00:00:11,080
No signs. Just the rule many countries
use: give way to the car on your right.

3
00:00:11,706 --> 00:00:15,346
Every driver looks right, and
sees a car they have to wait for.

4
00:00:15,666 --> 00:00:17,426
So every driver waits.

5
00:00:18,161 --> 00:00:20,021
Nobody is breaking the rule.

6
00:00:20,541 --> 00:00:22,021
Everybody is following it perfectly.

7
00:00:22,251 --> 00:00:24,981
And nobody will ever move.

8
00:00:26,064 --> 00:00:28,484
Computer scientists call this a deadlock.

9
00:00:28,564 --> 00:00:31,344
And it doesn't only happen on roads.

10
00:00:32,024 --> 00:00:36,684
It happens inside databases, operating
systems and ordinary software.

11
00:00:37,385 --> 00:00:41,025
Computers are often good at spotting
a deadlock once it has happened.

12
00:00:41,865 --> 00:00:44,005
But could a program read
another program and tell

13
00:00:44,235 --> 00:00:47,245
you, for certain, whether
it will ever get stuck?

14
00:00:47,873 --> 00:00:49,713
For many programs, yes.

15
00:00:49,933 --> 00:00:54,033
But a method that works for every
possible program can't exist.

16
00:00:54,493 --> 00:00:57,633
And not because computers
aren't fast enough yet.

17
00:00:58,033 --> 00:01:00,993
No faster computer will ever change it.

18
00:01:03,483 --> 00:01:05,583
Here's how a program gets stuck.

19
00:01:06,343 --> 00:01:10,123
Imagine an online shop, with two
tasks running at the same time.

20
00:01:10,706 --> 00:01:12,986
Before a task changes anything, it takes a

21
00:01:13,216 --> 00:01:16,186
lock on it, so that
nobody else can touch it.

22
00:01:16,826 --> 00:01:19,066
Like taking the only key to a room.

23
00:01:19,657 --> 00:01:23,757
The first task locks a customer's
account, then reaches for their order.

24
00:01:24,557 --> 00:01:27,557
The second locks the order,
then reaches for the account.

25
00:01:28,310 --> 00:01:30,890
One holds the account
and waits for the order.

26
00:01:31,570 --> 00:01:34,210
The other holds the order
and waits for the account.

27
00:01:34,970 --> 00:01:37,250
Neither will let go of what it's holding.

28
00:01:37,903 --> 00:01:42,583
And the strange part is, this code can
run a million times without any trouble.

29
00:01:43,423 --> 00:01:48,603
Only when the two tasks overlap in exactly
the wrong order does everything stop.

30
00:01:49,180 --> 00:01:51,060
The bug was there all along.

31
00:01:51,480 --> 00:01:53,540
The timing just exposed it.

32
00:01:54,278 --> 00:01:57,758
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

33
00:01:58,378 --> 00:01:59,978
It really helps the channel.

34
00:02:02,368 --> 00:02:05,808
Databases run into this all
the time, so they keep watch.

35
00:02:06,248 --> 00:02:08,948
They draw a map of who
is waiting for whom.

36
00:02:09,506 --> 00:02:11,286
The first waits for the second.

37
00:02:11,366 --> 00:02:13,746
The second waits for the first.

38
00:02:14,206 --> 00:02:17,606
When the arrows close into a loop,
that's a deadlock. No guessing.

39
00:02:18,006 --> 00:02:19,586
The loop is right there.

40
00:02:19,889 --> 00:02:22,269
Then the database does something brutal.

41
00:02:22,929 --> 00:02:25,889
It picks one of the tasks
and throws its work away.

42
00:02:26,579 --> 00:02:30,019
Microsoft's SQL Server even
tells the loser that it has

43
00:02:30,249 --> 00:02:33,979
been chosen as the deadlock
victim, and should try again.

44
00:02:34,547 --> 00:02:38,507
The other task carries on, and
the victim can simply try again.

45
00:02:39,123 --> 00:02:43,723
Notice what that does and
doesn't fix. The jam is cleared.

46
00:02:44,323 --> 00:02:45,643
But the code that caused it is still

47
00:02:45,873 --> 00:02:48,503
there, waiting for the
next unlucky moment.

48
00:02:49,500 --> 00:02:53,280
So spotting a deadlock that has
already happened is the easy part.

49
00:02:54,040 --> 00:02:56,180
The hard question is a different one.

50
00:02:56,702 --> 00:03:00,922
Not: is this program stuck right
now? But: give it this input.

51
00:03:01,382 --> 00:03:02,842
Could it ever get stuck?

52
00:03:03,458 --> 00:03:05,698
The first is a photo of the present.

53
00:03:06,358 --> 00:03:07,898
You can just look at it.

54
00:03:08,538 --> 00:03:11,698
The second is a prediction
about every possible future.

55
00:03:12,254 --> 00:03:17,774
And programs make that very hard. They
make decisions. They repeat things.

56
00:03:18,234 --> 00:03:22,074
They can run calculations that
take a second, or a thousand years.

57
00:03:22,723 --> 00:03:25,043
A program might get stuck
at once, after a million

58
00:03:25,273 --> 00:03:28,483
steps, for one rare
input, or never at all.

59
00:03:29,005 --> 00:03:30,845
You could just run it and watch.

60
00:03:31,365 --> 00:03:34,985
But if it hasn't got stuck after
an hour, what does that tell you?

61
00:03:35,525 --> 00:03:38,905
Nothing. It might get stuck
in the next second, or never.

62
00:03:41,335 --> 00:03:45,855
In 1936, a young mathematician
called Alan Turing showed that

63
00:03:46,085 --> 00:03:50,235
some questions about programs
can't be answered by any program.

64
00:03:50,675 --> 00:03:53,215
The most famous one sounds even simpler.

65
00:03:53,806 --> 00:03:55,966
Take any program, and its input.

66
00:03:56,666 --> 00:03:59,766
Will it eventually stop?
Or will it run forever?

67
00:04:00,541 --> 00:04:02,041
It's now called the halting problem.

68
00:04:02,271 --> 00:04:06,401
And no program can answer
it correctly in every case.

69
00:04:07,324 --> 00:04:10,284
The idea behind the proof
is surprisingly short.

70
00:04:10,984 --> 00:04:14,124
Suppose someone hands you
a perfect halting checker.

71
00:04:14,611 --> 00:04:17,091
Now you write a contrary little program.

72
00:04:17,831 --> 00:04:21,031
It asks the checker about itself,
and then does the opposite.

73
00:04:21,535 --> 00:04:24,235
If the checker says it will
stop, it loops forever.

74
00:04:24,935 --> 00:04:28,095
If the checker says it will
run forever, it stops at once.

75
00:04:28,822 --> 00:04:31,722
Whatever the checker says
about it, the checker is wrong.

76
00:04:32,342 --> 00:04:34,462
So a perfect checker can't exist.

77
00:04:37,132 --> 00:04:40,732
So what does this have to do
with deadlocks? Everything.

78
00:04:41,739 --> 00:04:45,359
Imagine someone claims to have
built a perfect deadlock predictor.

79
00:04:46,399 --> 00:04:49,419
Give it any program and its
input, and it always tells

80
00:04:49,649 --> 00:04:53,059
you, correctly, whether that
program will ever get stuck.

81
00:04:53,677 --> 00:04:56,117
Let's use it to solve the halting problem.

82
00:04:57,024 --> 00:05:00,804
Take any program you like, and
wrap it inside a new program.

83
00:05:01,472 --> 00:05:06,012
First, the new program simply runs the
old one, step by step, on a single thread.

84
00:05:06,452 --> 00:05:08,872
No locks. Nothing shared.

85
00:05:09,232 --> 00:05:11,092
Nothing that could ever get stuck.

86
00:05:11,385 --> 00:05:15,405
Then, if the old program ever
finishes, the new one starts two tasks.

87
00:05:16,345 --> 00:05:18,825
Each grabs one lock,
waits until the other has

88
00:05:19,055 --> 00:05:21,565
grabbed its own, and then
reaches for the other's.

89
00:05:22,325 --> 00:05:24,765
A deadlock, on purpose, every time.

90
00:05:25,330 --> 00:05:30,250
If the old program never finishes, the
new one never gets that far. No deadlock.

91
00:05:30,480 --> 00:05:31,680
Ever.

92
00:05:32,113 --> 00:05:36,033
So the new program gets stuck if,
and only if, the old one stops.

93
00:05:38,383 --> 00:05:40,963
Now ask the predictor
about the new program.

94
00:05:41,723 --> 00:05:44,923
Yes, it will deadlock,
means the old program stops.

95
00:05:45,543 --> 00:05:47,183
No means it runs forever.

96
00:05:47,913 --> 00:05:50,093
We've just solved the halting problem.

97
00:05:50,173 --> 00:05:52,913
And that's exactly what can't be done.

98
00:05:53,379 --> 00:05:56,679
So the perfect deadlock
predictor can't exist either.

99
00:05:57,293 --> 00:06:01,033
The trouble isn't locks, or
databases, or bad programmers.

100
00:06:01,713 --> 00:06:05,253
A program can hide any calculation
in front of a deadlock.

101
00:06:05,553 --> 00:06:08,653
To predict the deadlock, you'd
have to predict the calculation.

102
00:06:12,393 --> 00:06:16,013
But hold on. A real
computer isn't infinite.

103
00:06:16,633 --> 00:06:19,053
Its memory has a limited number of states.

104
00:06:19,153 --> 00:06:21,453
So couldn't you just check them all?

105
00:06:22,085 --> 00:06:25,405
For a machine with a fixed amount
of memory, in principle, yes.

106
00:06:26,065 --> 00:06:27,705
In practice, count them.

107
00:06:28,252 --> 00:06:32,212
The memory of a laptop with eight
gigabytes can be arranged in so many

108
00:06:32,442 --> 00:06:37,172
ways that just writing that number
down takes about twenty billion digits.

109
00:06:37,969 --> 00:06:42,009
The number of atoms in the
observable universe has about eighty.

110
00:06:42,652 --> 00:06:45,132
So on a real machine,
checking everything is

111
00:06:45,362 --> 00:06:47,952
possible in theory, and
hopeless in practice.

112
00:06:48,921 --> 00:06:50,421
But that's a different wall.

113
00:06:50,941 --> 00:06:53,441
Turing's limit isn't
about too many states.

114
00:06:54,201 --> 00:06:57,501
It applies to programs that
can always ask for more memory.

115
00:06:58,221 --> 00:07:02,141
For those, no perfect predictor can
exist, however big the computer.

116
00:07:05,171 --> 00:07:08,591
Does that make deadlocks hopeless? No.

117
00:07:08,771 --> 00:07:11,191
It means there's no perfect checker.

118
00:07:11,271 --> 00:07:14,771
So programmers write programs
that are easier to check.

119
00:07:15,428 --> 00:07:17,328
The simplest trick is an order.

120
00:07:17,908 --> 00:07:20,888
Every task must lock the
account before the order.

121
00:07:21,588 --> 00:07:23,288
Never the other way round.

122
00:07:23,655 --> 00:07:26,355
If every task really sticks
to that order, the loop

123
00:07:26,585 --> 00:07:29,835
can't form, and this kind
of deadlock can't happen.

124
00:07:30,764 --> 00:07:34,364
Other systems give up after a
timeout, take all their locks in

125
00:07:34,594 --> 00:07:38,744
one go, or let the database pick
a victim and simply try again.

126
00:07:39,151 --> 00:07:41,671
And for small, carefully
described designs,

127
00:07:41,751 --> 00:07:44,211
tools can check every possible state.

128
00:07:45,211 --> 00:07:47,931
Engineers at Amazon used
one to find a bug that took

129
00:07:48,161 --> 00:07:51,891
thirty-five steps, in exactly
the right order, to appear.

130
00:07:52,739 --> 00:07:55,959
These tools work because the
problem has been made smaller.

131
00:07:56,839 --> 00:08:01,099
They check a model of the system, not
every program that could ever exist.

132
00:08:02,240 --> 00:08:04,200
For real code, though, a checker that

133
00:08:04,430 --> 00:08:06,900
takes any program has
to give something up.

134
00:08:07,520 --> 00:08:10,040
It may warn about deadlocks
that can't happen, miss

135
00:08:10,270 --> 00:08:13,600
some that can, or sometimes
give no answer at all.

136
00:08:14,157 --> 00:08:17,737
A checker that never cries wolf,
never misses one, and always gives

137
00:08:17,967 --> 00:08:21,817
an answer, for every program, is
exactly what Turing ruled out.

138
00:08:24,737 --> 00:08:28,157
Back at the crossroads, the four
drivers don't sit there forever.

139
00:08:28,865 --> 00:08:34,385
Somebody waves. One driver gives up their
turn, and the jam is over in seconds.

140
00:08:35,528 --> 00:08:37,588
That's what the database does, too.

141
00:08:38,228 --> 00:08:39,888
It doesn't predict the jam.

142
00:08:40,568 --> 00:08:43,188
It notices it, and makes someone give way.

143
00:08:44,399 --> 00:08:46,819
Some problems need a faster computer.

144
00:08:47,479 --> 00:08:49,299
Some need a better idea.

145
00:08:49,737 --> 00:08:52,977
And some, like this one, have
no perfect solution at all.

146
00:08:53,517 --> 00:08:55,907
Only good rules, and
someone ready to wave.
